::ALG1_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>In the expression 5x + 8, what is the coefficient? {
~A. x#This identifies the variable, not the coefficient. The coefficient is the number multiplied by the variable.
=B. 5#Correct. In 5x + 8, the 5 is multiplied by x, so 5 is the coefficient.
~C. 8#This identifies the constant. The constant stands alone and is not multiplied by the variable.
~D. 5x#This identifies a term. The coefficient is the numerical factor of the variable term.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>In the expression 7n + 3, what is the constant? {
~A. 7#This is the coefficient because it is multiplied by n. The constant stands alone.
~B. n#This is the variable. A constant is a number that does not change.
=C. 3#Correct. The 3 stands alone, so it is the constant.
~D. 7n#This is a term containing the variable. The constant is the standalone number.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>A delivery company charges $6 per package plus a $10 fee. In 6p + 10, what does p represent? {
=A. the number of packages#Correct. The 6 is the cost per package, so p represents how many packages there are.
~B. the total fee#This describes the whole expression 6p + 10, not the variable p.
~C. the $10 fee#This describes the constant. The variable p changes with the number of packages.
~D. the cost per package#This describes the coefficient 6, not the variable p.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>In 4(x + 2), which part can be viewed as one grouped quantity? {
~A. 4#The 4 is a factor outside the parentheses. It is not the grouped quantity.
~B. x#This is only part of the grouped quantity. The parentheses group x and 2 together.
~C. 2#This is only part of the grouped quantity. The full grouped quantity is inside the parentheses.
=D. x + 2#Correct. The parentheses show that x + 2 should be viewed as one quantity.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L05_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression has two terms? {
~A. 8x#This expression has one term because there is no addition or subtraction separating parts.
=B. 3a + 5#Correct. The terms are 3a and 5, separated by addition.
~C. 4(m + 1)#This is a product with a grouped quantity. It is not written as two separate terms at the outer level.
~D. 12#This expression has one constant term.
}
